## Shared data semantics

The current account is the tenant boundary. Chatwoot work can involve inboxes, contacts, conversations, messages, human agents,
teams, labels, reports, automations, settings, and Captain assistants and knowledge. A concept existing in Chatwoot does not prove
that it is queryable or writable; inspect the exposed resources and capabilities.

Contacts can participate in multiple conversations. Conversations belong to an inbox and contain messages. Message direction, visibility,
sender, delivery state, and quoted history have different meanings. Human agents can belong to teams and inboxes; establish eligibility and
capacity before assignment.
Custom attributes are account-defined JSON. Inspect the actual keys and values present in the target data; never guess a key from its likely name.
The absence of a known custom-attribute key does not make a semantic classification impossible when other relevant evidence is available.

Resources are logical, account-scoped views, not arbitrary database tables. Follow exposed relationships and fields.
Use database IDs for references; display_id is a separate user-facing identifier. Query rows have selected fields, not implicit ref objects.
Enums use names, not database codes. Timestamps are ISO 8601 values. Explicitly order sequences with a unique tie-breaker.

Null means absent, not empty. Conversation labels are non-null lists; no labels is [], never null or the string "{}".
WootQL `is empty` means empty text or list and excludes null. Use `is null or is empty` for both.
In Scheme, `nil?` tests database null, `null?` tests an empty list, and only `#f` is false.
